    {
      "document_id": "262",
      "explanation": "A WWII-era essay on offensive vs. defensive war psychology and national complacency. Falls under the national defense/military preparedness exclusion; firearms regulation is never mentioned.",
      "label": "0"
    },
    {
      "document_id": "263",
      "explanation": "A Bicentennial piece on privately owned Revolutionary War muskets and the heritage of American arms ownership. Its jabs at 'fools who rail against private firearms ownership' are incidental rhetoric; the primary subject is firearms history and collecting, an excluded category, and no law or regulatory proposal is discussed.",
      "label": "0"
    },
    {
      "document_id": "264",
      "explanation": "Describes the support work of soldiers and Marines at the Camp Perry National Matches. Competitions/National Matches are excluded; no regulatory content.",
      "label": "0"
    },
    {
      "document_id": "265",
      "explanation": "About large-scale Army maneuvers and the importance of individual marksmanship training and the National Matches. Falls within the marksmanship-training and national-defense exclusions; no discussion of civilian firearms law.",
      "label": "0"
    },
    {
      "document_id": "266",
      "explanation": "About reaction time and older shooters remaining competitive—a marksmanship topic with no regulatory content. Excluded category.",
      "label": "0"
    },
    {
      "document_id": "267",
      "explanation": "The editorial centrally engages the gun-control debate: it attacks those who would 'abolish the Second Amendment' and 'take away all guns and stop the killing,' arguing instead for war on criminals. Under the definition, advocacy against measures to control civilian possession counts even without a specific bill, and the boundary rule is met because engaging the regulation debate is central to the argument. Ambiguity: much of the piece is a general liberty/crime meditation, but the anti-disarmament argument is a through-line, not a passing mention.",
