    },
    {
      "document_id": "271",
      "explanation": "About NRA membership growth, finances, and five-year planning. The references to 'legislators and gun control lobbyists' and legislative priorities are incidental framing; the negative clarification excludes NRA internal governance, membership, and fundraising texts.",
      "label": "0"
    },
    {
      "document_id": "272",
      "explanation": "Substantially discusses a CPSC lawsuit/administrative action that 'could spell the end of BB gun and air rifle manufacture and sales'—government action to control manufacture and sale—plus British licensing, registration, confiscation, and the Gun Control Network's proposals. The editorial explicitly frames the CPSC action as backdoor gun control (definition items on administrative rules and government policies; clarification items 2-4). Ambiguity: airguns are arguably not 'firearms,' but the text's substantive subject is government control of guns, so it is classified 1.",
      "label": "1"
    },
    {
      "document_id": "273",
      "explanation": "Entirely about the Brady law's National Instant Check System: FBI record-retention rules, statutory record-destruction requirements (18 USC 922), executive actions on gun-show transfers, litigation, and Project Exile enforcement. Matches clarification items (2), (3), and (5)—registration/record-keeping, administrative rulemaking, and enforcement framed within the gun-control debate.",
      "label": "1"
    },
    {
      "document_id": "274",
      "explanation": "Praise for Culver Military Academy and citizen-soldier military education. National defense/military preparedness exclusion; no firearms-regulation content.",
      "label": "0"
    },
    {
      "document_id": "275",
      "explanation": "Primarily an accounting of NRA expenses, a defense of the member firearms-insurance benefit against Jack Anderson's column, and a note about AMA members. Internal governance/membership/programs are excluded; the mention of the AMA's anti-gun statements is incidental to the organizational subject.",
      "label": "0"
    },
    {
      "document_id": "276",
      "explanation": "An analysis comparing non-firearm murder rates internationally to show that 'rigid restriction or elimination of civilian firearms fails to eliminate or vastly reduce murder.' The definition expressly includes analysis of the effectiveness of gun-control measures, and the piece directly addresses foreign restriction regimes.",
      "label": "1"
    },
    {
      "document_id": "277",
      "explanation": "Coverage of the U.S. rifle team's 1930 International Matches victory. Shooting competition is an excluded category; no regulatory content.",
      "label": "0"
    },
    {
      "document_id": "278",
      "explanation": "Explains NRA's revenue/expense breakdown versus other pro-gun groups. Internal governance/finances are excluded; no substantive gun-law discussion.",
      "label": "0"
    },
    {
      "document_id": "279",
      "explanation": "Celebrates 1993 ballot victories for 'three strikes,' bail denial, and prison funding—criminal-justice/sentencing policy. The single taunt about 'bizarre gun control schemes' is incidental. The negative clarification excludes general crime and sentencing policy without a gun-law focus.",
      "label": "0"
    },
    {
      "document_id": "280",
      "explanation": "A wartime essay on democracy, free speech, and the citizen's right to criticize officials. One phrase about police wanting citizens 'disarmed' is incidental; the text is not about firearms law.",
      "label": "0"
    },
    {
      "document_id": "281",
      "explanation": "Reports on NRA program expansion (hunter clinics, women's safety programs, sporting clays, CrimeStrike) and membership growth. Falls under the internal governance/membership/programs exclusion; the Second Amendment reference is incidental.",
      "label": "0"
    },
    {
      "document_id": "282",
      "explanation": "Recap of the successful 1930 National Matches and firing schools. Competitions/National Matches exclusion; no regulatory content.",
      "label": "0"
    },
    {
      "document_id": "283",
      "explanation": "Although much of the editorial attacks Clinton-Gore on campaign finance and the IRS audit, it substantively engages gun regulation: HUD Secretary Cuomo's leadership of 'Federal efforts to sue lawful firearms commerce into oblivion' (clarification item 4 on backdoor gun-control litigation), the cabinet-sanctioned national gun-control grassroots campaign, and Reno's focus on gun numbers. Specific gun-law content is central enough under the boundary rule to distinguish it from pure electioneering.",
      "label": "1"
    },
    {
      "document_id": "284",
      "explanation": "A miscellaneous NRA update: membership growth, post-riot safety training, ranges, and programs. The brief notes about ILA's role in defeating an anti-gun congressman and the NBC/Second Amendment campaign are incidental items within an organizational report. Under the boundary rule, the main subject is NRA programs and membership (excluded), so 0. Ambiguity: the political items are present but not the text's central argument.",
      "label": "0"
    },
    {
      "document_id": "285",
      "explanation": "Detailed critique of H.R. 16250 (Mikva), which would ban handguns from interstate commerce, impose government-licensed club lockups, storage mandates, fees, inspections, and compensated surrender. A specific bill controlling sale, possession, and storage—clarification items (1) and (2). Paradigmatic positive.",
      "label": "1"
    },
